      {
       "id": "0001024",
       "elType": "widget",
       "widgetType": "accordion",
       "settings": {
        "tabs": [
         {
          "tab_title": "Task 1 — Reproduce the Bug",
          "tab_content": "<p>Run the current dashboard query (<code>starter/dashboard_query.sql</code>, provided as-is — don't edit it) and compare its row count to the number of distinct orders. The dashboard returns more rows than there are orders, and a noticeably higher total revenue. Confirm this for yourself before moving on.</p>",
          "_id": "000101f"
         },
         {
          "tab_title": "Task 2 — Find the Grain Problem",
          "tab_content": "<p>Query <code>orders</code> directly. Some order_ids appear 2 or 3 times — look at their status, updated_at and order_version. The table isn't at one-row-per-order grain; it's at one-row-per-status-update grain, and that's exactly what fans out when it's joined to order_items.</p>",
          "_id": "0001020"
         },
         {
          "tab_title": "Task 3 — Deduplicate to One Row per Order",
          "tab_content": "<p>Keep only the latest version of each order. Two things will trip up a naive <code>ORDER BY updated_at DESC</code>: updated_at is NULL on an order's very first row, and at least one order has two rows with the identical updated_at (a replayed webhook), while another has two rows that are both NULL. Use <code>COALESCE(updated_at, order_ts)</code> plus order_version as a tie-break.</p><p><strong>Hint:</strong> Partition by order_id, order by the coalesced timestamp then version, keep rn = 1.</p>",
          "_id": "0001021"
         },
         {
          "tab_title": "Task 4 — Fix the Join Instead of the Symptom",
          "tab_content": "<p>Even with orders deduplicated, joining it straight to order_items and grouping is fragile. Aggregate order_items to order grain first, then join that 1:1 to your deduplicated orders. One line item has a NULL unit_price — SUM() silently drops it, so use <code>COALESCE(unit_price, 0)</code> and add a has_missing_price flag instead of letting the gap disappear unexplained.</p><p><strong>Hint:</strong> Aggregate order_items to order grain in its own step before joining anything to it.</p>",
          "_id": "0001022"
         },
         {
          "tab_title": "Task 5 — Assemble and Verify the Trusted Query",
          "tab_content": "<p>Combine Tasks 3 and 4 into one query in <code>starter/solution.sql</code> returning exactly one row per order_id, with columns order_id, status, revenue, has_missing_price. Then run the automated check: <code>python tests/test_solution.py</code>. It checks required columns, one-row-per-order grain, and an exact match against the trusted revenue and status for every order — and exits non-zero if any check fails.</p>",
          "_id": "0001023"
         }
        ],
        "title_color": "#14151A",
        "content_color": "#54575F",
        "border_color": "#E7E3D9"
       },
       "elements": []
